  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nStrong upper level ridging stretching across the southern half\nof the country is set to undergo a bit of a transformation today\nthat will amplify shower and storm chances for the Ohio Valley\ninto the Mid-Atlantic. This modification to the upper level\npattern will suppress surface high pressure that hovered\noverhead the past few days, opening the door for a wider area \nof shower and storm chances here in the Central Appalachians. \n\nA stalled frontal boundary draped across the Great Lakes region\nhas been the catalyst behind the rounds of thunderstorm \ncomplexes that has swept through the Upper Ohio Valley towards \nthe Delmarva coast each day, only sliding through our far \nnorthern zones as a result of the surface high parked to the \nsouth. With this feature now eroding, these complexes will be \ncapable of spreading across more of the forecast area, and could\ncome in multiple waves today, tonight, and into Saturday. Hi-res\nCAMs tend to struggle with timing out storms and mapping their\nprojected paths within this regime, so radar and satellite \ntrends will be monitored for future forecast updates.\n\nAnother hot and muggy day on tap for today will be conducive for\napproaching complexes to maintain their composure as they drift\nsouth to southeastward, yielding potential for strong to severe\nwind gusts along the way. The day one severe outlook has \nexpanded the slight risk further west into the CWA today, \ncapturing the wider scheme of storm capability with the loss of \nthe surface high. The risk for convection spills into tonight as\nepisodic rounds of showers and storms evolve off of the stalled\nboundary. \n\nThe front is then slated to travel southward into the vicinity \nof the Central Appalachians on Saturday, with on and off chances\nof showers and storms prevailing into the start of the weekend.\nThis should gradually shift the severe risk south throughout its\njourney towards the southeastern US, wrapping up altogether over\nthe forecast area heading into Sunday. From there, a stretch of\ndrier weather is set to reign over the region through the first\nhalf of the work week.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe ongoing heat wave remains firmly in place today, causing\noppressive afternoon and evening conditions. A Heat Advisory in\neffect stretches into this evening as a result. Central \nguidance forecast heat indices are in line with yesterday's \nobserved values, with locations across the Tri-State area \nstretching into the 103 to 105 range. \n\nThere is still some unknowns with temperature spread and \nresulting heat index values for Saturday as we await the cold \nfrontal passage. Additionally, latest model runs suggest an \nuptick in showers and storms throughout the day Saturday that \ncould keep the heat and humidity below advisory thresholds. \nEnough uncertainty exists to currently refrain from an extension\nto the Heat Advisory with this forecast issuance.\n\nOnce the frontal passage takes place this weekend, a relief from\nthe heat prevails heading into the start of next week.\nTemperatures will tumble down closer to their climatological\nnorms for this time of year, but could be fleeting as long term\nmodels hint at the return of the heat and humidity by the middle\nof next week.",
